AB  - We propose GLAD, a Graph-LLM Augmented Framework for Out-of-Scope Intent Detection, to improve both in-scope (IS) and out-of-scope (OOS) intent classification in task-oriented dialogue systems. Unlike prior methods that either ignore inter-intent structure or rely too heavily on large language models (LLMs), GLAD injects LLM-generated intent prototypes into a lightweight dynamic graph for relational reasoning. The framework builds a prototype graph from intent descriptions and refines it during training with hysteresis-based edge updates and exponential moving averages. GLAD further introduces a multi-level contrastive objective that aligns utterances with class prototypes, tightens within-class cohesion, and expands decision margins around confusing intents. At inference time, query-aware subgraph sampling supports efficient graph-based reasoning, while a cost-aware hybrid routing mechanism calls an LLM-based verifier only for low-confidence cases. Experiments on three public benchmarks show that GLAD remains highly competitive overall, delivers the strongest F1-OPEN at every tested coverage level, and is particularly effective in low-coverage, fine-grained settings while keeping LLM usage low. Code, configs, prompts, and reproducibility notes are publicly available at https://github.com/Big10tou/GLAD-OOSID.
