AB  - Current decision-making systems of autonomous driving struggle to balance safety, environmental adaptability, interpretability, and real-time performance in complex dynamic traffic environments. To overcome this issue, this study presents a dual-loop self-learning framework named DuSA. This approach integrates the advantages of rule-based constraints, reinforcement learning (RL), and large language models (LLMs) to construct a collaborative decision-making mechanism comprising an RL optimization loop and an LLM optimization loop. Specifically, the RL agent is responsible for learning efficient decision-making policies through interaction with the environment. Rather than directly participating in online decision-making, the LLM serves as a generation and iteration engine for safety filters, ensuring reliable, dynamic safety constraints for the decision-making process. Furthermore, by incorporating a policy distillation mechanism that combines imitation learning and reward reshaping, the knowledge from the safety filters is gradually internalized into the policy network. This mechanism significantly enhances policy convergence efficiency and decision-making quality while reducing reliance on external safety filters. Tests on highway and ramp merge scenarios show that DuSA achieves nearly 100% task success, enhances learning efficiency by 46.0%–49.3% relative to baselines, and maintains robust performance under high-density traffic. Moreover, the average decision-making time per step of this method is merely 0.03 s, fully satisfying the real-time requirements of online autonomous driving decision-making. The research results demonstrate that DuSA can effectively enhance the safety, environmental adaptability, reliability, and deployability of autonomous driving decision-making systems in complex traffic scenarios, thereby providing a novel technical pathway for constructing safe and trustworthy intelligent driving decision-making methods.
