AB  - Protein design is treated here as the inverse problem of biophysics: finding sequences that stabilize a desired structural or functional ensemble over competing states. The chapter frames success in terms of free-energy landscapes, emphasizing stability gaps, competing basins, configurational entropy, designability, alphabet size, and negative design. It shows how heteropolymer theory identifies the conditions under which folds can be encoded and why directional interactions and explicit treatment of alternative states are central. The chapter then follows the evolution from coarse-grained and atomistic design methods to modern learning-based workflows, including structure-conditioned sequence design, diffusion-based backbone generation, protein language models, and relaxed-sequence optimization. AI methods are presented as powerful priors and search accelerators rather than replacements for physical reasoning. Across folds, binders, enzymes, switches, and synthetic systems, the central argument remains that reliable design is best understood as free-energy landscape engineering, with the strongest future lying in hybrid AI–physics strategies coupled to experiment.
