AB  - Vision-language models (VLMs) jointly process and align visual and textual information. In medical imaging, VLMs have shown potential to support diagnostic interpretation, automate clinical tasks, and improve the interaction between visual information and clinical language. This review provides a detailed analysis of 76 studies published between 2020 and August 2026. First, we introduce the foundations of medical vision-language modeling and discuss the main challenges associated with integrating medical images and clinical text. We then organize applications into five clinical tasks, namely medical visual question answering, radiology report generation, language-guided segmentation, anomaly detection and disease diagnosis, and cross-modal retrieval, and discuss multimodal large language models and instruction-tuned systems as capabilities that span these tasks. Publicly used datasets, benchmarks, and evaluation methodologies are subsequently examined, followed by a discussion of current limitations and future research directions, with particular attention to clinical reliability, interpretability, grounding, and robustness.
