AB  - Sentiment analysis in low-resource Southeast Asian languages has attracted increasing attention because many languages still lack annotated corpora, linguistic tools, and standardized evaluation benchmarks. In this survey, we focus on the main dimensions of low-resource sentiment analysis, including available resources, major methodological families, comparative evaluation, and practical deployment challenges across Vietnamese, Thai, Indonesian, Malay, Khmer, Lao, and Burmese/Myanmar. We review lexicon-based, classical machine learning, neural, transformer-based, cross-lingual, and recent large language model approaches. We also examine how language characteristics, domain variation, and supervision settings affect model effectiveness, and discuss open issues such as benchmark fragmentation, reproducibility, and cross-domain robustness. The central critical insight is that low-resource sentiment analysis in Southeast Asia should be judged not only by model performance, but also by resource ecology, evaluation comparability, and deployment realism.
