AB  - Financial inclusion of farmers still remains a challenge due to complex risk factors and inequitable access to technology, leading to biased and inconsistent loan recommendations. This review aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the use of AI and ML to enhance agricultural loan recommendations. Building on the flow of a typical ML pipeline: Problem Understanding & Feature Specification, Field Data Acquisition, and Model Design, Training, and Deployment, the review identifies factors influencing agricultural loan recommendations, state-of-the-art data acquisition methods to feed and train the models, and finally the various statistical, AI-based and other techniques for building robust loan recommendation models. These models would in turn help financial institutions assess loan default risk, enabling more loans to be issued to smallholder farmers excluded from the system. PRISMA 2020 guidelines were used to perform the systematic literature review. Google Scholar, Science Direct and IEEE Xplore were searched. A total of 293 unique records were identified, and after screening and manual search additions, 214 met the criteria and were included in the analysis. This review summarizes data acquisition technologies such as UAVs, GIS, IoT, and edge devices, along with a critical evaluation of the latest techniques, including deep learning, machine learning, regression, PCA, fuzzy logic, and feature engineering methods. By highlighting the appropriate application of each technology and providing actionable insights, this review offers a unique contribution in agricultural loan recommendations research. Directions for future research have been identified, including bias mitigation, data interoperability, and model interpretability to boost equitable financial access and widespread adoption of AI/ML in the agricultural credit ecosystem.
