AB  - End-to-end (E2E) autonomous driving models can achieve strong benchmark performance while still relying on spurious correlations, which creates a need for causal safety auditing. Existing interpretability methods are often passive, whereas active counterfactual explanation methods typically require manual object selection and mainly target discrete decisions. This paper proposes VLM-driven causal auditing (VICA) for continuous control E2E policies. VICA pairs each natural observation with an object absence counterfactual generated by the Segment Anything Model and Large Mask Inpainting, and evaluates the frozen policy’s continuous control response against a counterfactual dynamics prior provided by a VLM. For continuous actions, VICA uses the causal deviation rate (CDR) and causal alignment score (CAS) to measure response magnitude and directional consistency against the prior. Across Bench2Drive interaction scenarios, VICA diagnoses model-specific failure profiles across UniAD, VAD, and TCP. Under the default prior, TCP shows a 64.15% diagnosed failure proportion, mainly through Misalignment and Hallucination, indicating weaker causal alignment in the audited scenarios. These results show that VICA can support automated causal safety analysis for E2E autonomous driving.
ER  - 

