AB  - High penetration of distributed energy resources exposes microgrid energy management systems (EMSs) to high-impact low-probability (HILP) contingencies, such as unscheduled islanding, communication degradation, and natural disasters. Conventional optimization-based EMSs usually rely on predefined parameters and fixed contingency settings, which limit their adaptability when emergency information appears in heterogeneous and unstructured forms. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a large language model (LLM)-enhanced energy management framework for event-adaptive resilient microgrid operation. The proposed framework separates the LLM-based cognitive layer from the numerical optimization layer, preventing the LLM from issuing direct physical control commands. Instead, a formal semantic–numeric parameterization interface is introduced to convert unstructured contingency descriptions into structured, bounded, and validated optimization parameters. In this way, contingency information can be reflected in risk-aware parameter updates, disturbance-response strategies, and service-restoration priorities. The generated parameters update the objectives and constraints of the EMS without modifying the underlying solver structure. For networked microgrid coordination, the multi-microgrid (MMG) scheduling problem is solved through an Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM)-based distributed framework. Case studies covering unscheduled islanding, communication degradation, natural disasters, Monte Carlo uncertainty tests, and a dual-disaster scenario show that the proposed approach improves resilience, maintains critical-load supply, and supports adaptive EMS decision-making under HILP contingency conditions.
