AB  - Although cement-based solidification of radioactive wastewater is a well-established technique, it suffers from high leaching rates, poor long-term stability, and weak anion immobilization capacity. To address these challenges, this work integrates the large language model DeepSeek with the bibliometric software VOSviewer, enabling rapid semantic retrieval and clustering of massive literature data, summarization of key terms, and construction of co-occurrence networks and other knowledge graphs. These analyses provide insights into research trends and emerging hotspots concerning the incorporation of layered double hydroxides (LDHs) into cement-based materials for the immobilization of anionic radionuclides. Building on this foundation, the paper systematically reviews the structural characteristics, modification strategies, and adsorption mechanisms of LDHs, offering guidance for the development of targeted modification approaches and the “precise capture and locking” of anions. Furthermore, different methods of integrating LDHs with cement matrices are discussed in detail, elucidating the mechanisms by which LDHs simultaneously enhance the physicochemical properties of cement-based materials and their capacity for anion immobilization, thereby clarifying the solidification mechanisms of LDHs–cement composites. Finally, a dual immobilization mechanism is proposed, and a future research framework is outlined for achieving the long-term and safe immobilization of radioactive anions.
