UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950584926003253
KW  - Data leakage
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Preprocessing
KW  - Jupyter notebooks
KW  - Static analysis
KW  - Graph neural networks
AB  - Context:
Data leakage is a well-recognized threat to the validity of machine learning evaluations. Prior work has proposed conceptual taxonomies that describe leakage at a high level of abstraction, while code-level detection approaches typically operationalize leakage using coarse-grained or binary labels. As a result, how leakage concretely manifests within real-world machine learning pipelines, and how abstract leakage notions map to code-level practices, remains insufficiently understood.
Objective:
This study aims to characterize how data leakage manifests in practice within real-world machine learning pipelines and to assess how effectively existing detection techniques capture these code-level manifestations.
Methods:
We curate a corpus of over 600 publicly available Jupyter notebooks from GitHub and Kaggle and perform fine-grained manual annotation at the level of individual training–evaluation instances, referred to as model pairs. Based on this annotation, we derive an empirically grounded, code-centric categorization of leakage manifestations, capturing the operations and implementation patterns through which leakage occurs. We further compare a graph-based learning detector, a state-of-the-art static-analysis detector, and an LLM-assisted hybrid detector, evaluating their performance both at the coarse leakage-type level and across the identified manifestations.
Results:
Our analysis shows that many leakage manifestations arise from common preprocessing operations, including categorical encoding, imputation, feature scaling, and text vectorization. Among preprocessing manifestations, we distinguish three types of evaluation information exposed to the training process: statistical, target, and feature-space information. Feature-space information emerges as particularly prevalent yet comparatively under-characterized, especially in categorical encoding. Detection results show that the LLM-assisted hybrid detector achieves the strongest overall performance, while static analysis remains effective for manifestations covered by predefined rules and the learning-based detector captures some unmodeled manifestations but struggles with sparsely represented cases.
Conclusion:
Data leakage in real-world machine learning pipelines primarily arises from recurring preprocessing and data-handling practices rather than rare implementation errors. By characterizing the code-level manifestations through which established leakage types are realized, our analysis bridges the gap between abstract leakage taxonomies and coarse-grained detection and provides a finer operational view of how leakage occurs in practice.
