AB  - Text-to-Image Person Re-identification (TI-ReID) aims to retrieve all images of a target individual from a gallery given a natural-language description, yet existing methods still rely on instance-level similarity from pretrained Vision–Language Models (VLMs), despite relevance being defined at the identity level. This instance–identity mismatch, together with the modality gap and large intra-identity appearance variations, makes cross-modal alignment fragile and leads to unstable rankings. We introduce a Prototype-Driven Enhancement (PDE) module that exploits identity-level structure in the image modality purely at inference: gallery features are clustered into identity prototypes, and the resulting Query–Prototype Similarity (QP-Similarity) is fused with conventional Query–Gallery Similarity (QG-Similarity) to refine retrieval scores. Building on this, a cascaded refinement scheme with adaptive prototype granularity yields a training-free, plug-and-play framework that can be seamlessly attached to VLM-based TI-ReID backbones without modifying their parameters or using additional data. Experiments on CUHK-PEDES, ICFG-PEDES, and RSTPReid show consistent improvements over strong baselines and establish new state-of-the-art performance on all three benchmarks.
