AB  - The global shift to renewable energy, crucial for addressing climate change and resource depletion, hinges on energy storage systems to manage the variability of wind and solar power, ensuring a reliable and stable energy supply for a sustainable future. However, the interconnections and interdependencies among physical energy infrastructures, societal information systems, and human behaviors necessitate the development of a parallel energy storage system based on Cyber-Physical-Social Systems (CPSS) and parallel intelligence. Recently, the exceptional performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) has sparked growing interest in their potential applications within energy storage systems. Therefore this chapter introduces the LLM-based parallel energy storage framework, which integrates three layers—storage, computing, and execution—along with biological, digital, and robotic humans to enable efficient task execution through information exchange and virtual-real interactions. Specifically, its basic architecture, operational process, and key enabling technologies are explored, alongside emerging trends such as shared energy storage, the multienergy internet, energy routers, and blockchain-as-a-service.
