AB  - Traffic perception using monocular surveillance cameras has been widely adopted in Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS). However, reliable perception of pedestrian crossing behavior in nighttime traffic scenes remains challenging due to low-light conditions and limited computational resources for edge deployment. To address these challenges, we propose a compute-efficient framework that leverages compact pedestrian behavioral descriptors and visual embeddings as intermediate representations to facilitate pedestrian crossing behavior recognition at road intersections in nighttime traffic videos. The proposed framework consists of two stages. In the first stage, a YOLOv11m object detector fine-tuned on a custom nighttime pedestrian behavior dataset is used to detect traffic participants, after which pedestrian instances are selected and corresponding behavioral descriptors are extracted. These descriptors include motion and interaction features that capture critical behavioral cues associated with pedestrian movement. In addition, regions of interest (ROI) corresponding to detected pedestrian bounding boxes are cropped from the original video frames and processed using a pretrained DINOv2 model to obtain visual embeddings. The extracted descriptors and visual embeddings are fused to form frame-level feature representations. In the second stage, the fused features from sequential frames are organized into temporal feature sequences for each pedestrian and processed by a lightweight transformer-based module to recognize pedestrian crossing behavior. Ablation studies and comparative analyses demonstrate that the proposed framework can effectively capture pedestrian crossing patterns and that it outperforms several state-of-the-art Vision-Language Models in both accuracy and efficiency under nighttime conditions.
