AB  - Nuclear instrumentation and control (I&C) software verification requires regulatory evidence that is accurate, traceable, auditable, and robust against AI-generated errors. LLMs can generate candidate constraints from regulatory text, but outputs are unverified and lack an audit trail for nuclear V&V review. NeuSys addresses this by treating LLM outputs as candidate constraints, passing them through a hallucination guard and scoped SMT-based consistency-checking layer (Z3) for numeric/temporal constraints. These stages form a Logic-Informed Safety Envelope reducing the risk of source-unsupported numeric thresholds or SMT-representable contradictions entering the audit-ready compliance-review record. NeuSysRAG extends this with retrieval-augmented generation and instruction-based scope separation to reduce context leakage. We construct GT-833, a copyright-aware tiered-release benchmark of 833 expert-validated requirements and 922 normalized constraints from 24 sources spanning five heterogeneous regulatory source categories organized across four hierarchy levels (10 CFR, NRC, IEEE/ANSI, IEC, EPRI), addressing a longstanding gap in nuclear regulatory NLP. On GT-833, NeuSys variants preserve LLM-level accuracy (F1 = 92.8–93.5%; NeuSys vs. Few-Shot p = 0.48, NeuSysRAG-IA vs. Few-Shot p = 0.07, McNemar's test) while adding audit trails, including guard verdicts and, for numeric/temporal types, SMT records. NeuSysRAG-IA attains the highest observed F1 (93.5%), a modest, suggestive gain (+0.23 pp. vs. LLM Few-Shot; p=0.07), and reduces false positives from 142 to 133. These results indicate safety-oriented regulatory NLP should be evaluated not only by aggregate F1 but also by whether constraints can be traced, guarded, and, where applicable, checked for consistency before entering the compliance record. NeuSys is a pre-verification support tool for qualified nuclear V&V engineers, not a replacement for human regulatory judgment or autonomous compliance authority.
