KW  - Human–chatbot interactions
KW  - Safety
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) and mental-health chatbots are quickly being used for psychoeducation, assessment/triage, and conversational support, but evidence, safety, and governance are unevenly reported. This chapter maps the application taxonomy to education, screening, and intervention; synthesizes reported clinical and engagement outcomes; and translates recurrent risks into an alignment-engineering and governance blueprint. Based on peer-reviewed, Scopus-indexed literature and recent implementations, we summarize signals on depressive and anxiety symptoms (e.g., PHQ-9, GAD-7), adherence/dropout, user experience, and cost considerations. In summary, short-term benefits are promising but heterogeneous; adherence is variable; safety and adverse-event reporting are inconsistent; and economic analyzes are sparse. We then review the risk landscape—hallucinations and unsafe advice, misuse and jailbreaks, bias and inequity, overdependence, and legal/regulatory gaps—and match each risk to specific mitigations: domain-specific fine-tuning with safety-aware reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), retrieval-augmented grounding, uncertainty-aware refusals and escalation, and layered explainability for users and developers. Building on these controls, we operationalize SAFEE (Safety, Accountability, Fairness, Explainability, Ethics) into implementable checks, team ownership, and audit trails. Finally, we present a staged evaluation pipeline—from preclinical validation to pilot, randomized efficacy, pragmatic implementation, and postmarket surveillance—with outcome decision criteria to standardize reporting of symptoms, engagement, harms, and costs. Taken together, we provide a practical playbook that combines the emerging evidence with alignment engineering and SAFEE-based governance to responsibly deploy and consistently evaluate therapeutic LLMs. LLMs and mental-health chatbots are quickly being used for psychoeducation, assessment/triage, and conversational support, but evidence, safety, and governance are unevenly reported. This chapter maps the application taxonomy to education, screening, and intervention; synthesizes reported clinical and engagement outcomes; and translates recurrent risks into an alignment-engineering and governance blueprint. Based on peer-reviewed, Scopus-indexed literature and recent implementations, we summarize signals on depressive and anxiety symptoms (e.g., PHQ-9, GAD-7), adherence/dropout, user experience, and cost considerations. In summary, short-term benefits are promising but heterogeneous; adherence is variable; safety and adverse-event reporting are inconsistent; and economic analyzes are sparse. We then review the risk landscape—hallucinations and unsafe advice, misuse and jailbreaks, bias and inequity, overdependence, and legal/regulatory gaps—and match each risk to specific mitigations: domain-specific fine-tuning with safety-aware RLHF, retrieval-augmented grounding, uncertainty-aware refusals and escalation, and layered explainability for users and developers. Building on these controls, we operationalize SAFEE (Safety, Accountability, Fairness, Explainability, Ethics) into implementable checks, team ownership, and audit trails. Finally, we present a staged evaluation pipeline—from preclinical validation to pilot, randomized efficacy, pragmatic implementation, and postmarket surveillance—with outcome decision criteria to standardize reporting of symptoms, engagement, harms, and costs. Taken together, we provide a practical playbook that combines the emerging evidence with alignment engineering and SAFEE-based governance to responsibly deploy and consistently evaluate therapeutic LLMs.
