AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have catalyzed a transformative paradigm shift in the auditing of User-Generated Content (UGC) and AI-Generated Content (AIGC), advancing content moderation systems from rule-based paradigms to cognition-driven architectures. Despite their promising capabilities, current LLM-based moderation frameworks predominantly rely on fine-tuning approaches, which encounter three critical limitations: (1) the heterogeneity and continuous evolution of safety standards across platforms and jurisdictions; (2) the prohibitive cost and limited accessibility of high-quality, domain-specific annotated datasets; and (3) the susceptibility to hallucinations coupled with insufficient decision traceability, undermining moderation reliability and auditability. To address these challenges comprehensively, this study introduces CM-MRAG, a general-purpose multimodal moderation framework that integrates fine-tuned multimodal LLMs with a flexible retrieval module. A base model is trained to detect harmful patterns in multimodal content, and the MRAG component is subsequently employed to enhance reasoning through relevant examples. This design enables rapid adaptation to new safety requirements without repeated fine-tuning. The approach reduces data and computational demands and improves reliability by mitigating hallucinations. Experimental results demonstrate that CM-MRAG reaches 97.92 % accuracy on in-distribution data and 93.07 % on out-of-distribution data, outperforming strong baselines such as Llama-Guard (93.20 %) and LlavaGuard-B (91.64 %). The ablation studies further indicate that scaling the MRAG repository consistently boosts accuracy, from 94.17 % to 99.17 %. In practice, CM-MRAG can be integrated into a wide range of applications, including social media platforms, online learning environments, and digital marketplaces. By maintaining reliable performance and low latency while adapting to evolving content patterns, it provides a practical solution for strengthening content security across diverse digital ecosystems. The implementation is available at https://github.com/clsyc/CMRAG.
