AB  - Robotic planning breaks down when commonsense reasoning is required to resolve linguistic ambiguity and to interpret objects correctly. To address this, we present ConceptBot, a modular planning framework that integrates large language models with knowledge graphs to jointly disambiguate instructions, ground object semantics, and support risk-aware task-level planning. ConceptBot comprises three components: (i) an Object Properties Extraction (OPE) module that augments scene understanding with semantic concepts from ConceptNet; (ii) a User Request Processing (URP) module that resolves ambiguities and structures free-form instructions; and (iii) a Planner that grounds the structured request into sequential, action-schema-compliant pick-and-place policies. On a 47-instruction symbolic planning benchmark, ConceptBot achieves 87.0% planning-policy correctness on implicit tasks, compared with 31.0% for SayCan, 27.0% for ReAct, and 13.0% for ProgPrompt, and 76.2% on risk-aware tasks, compared with 15.0%, 37.5%, and 22.5%, respectively. These values quantify symbolic policy correctness rather than end-to-end robot success. Separately, controlled PyBullet and Franka executions demonstrate integration of the generated policies with simulation and physical manipulation stacks. On SafeAgentBench, ConceptBot obtains an observed overall BSS of 80%, without domain-specific training.
