AB  - Climate change and its compounding abiotic stresses are outpacing the capacity of conventional breeding to deliver resilient cereal cultivars. Traditional pipelines for donor-line identification capture only limited dimensions of performance and rarely represent the combined effects of genetic, environmental and management factors. Artificial intelligence (AI) offers a promising direction, yet current algorithms remain constrained in accuracy, scalability and generalisability across multi-environment and multi-omics datasets. To date, no framework has unified holo-omics with genotype (G), environment (E), management (M) and holobiome (H) information within machine-learning, deep-learning and large-language-model systems. In this review, we trace the field from classical multi-environment trials through genomic–enviromic integration, high-throughput phenomics and holo-omics toward systems-level AI. Building on this synthesis, we propose a conceptual G × E × M × H predictive framework (not yet experimentally validated). It integrates microbiome-modulated crop growth models with advanced AI methods (Hadamard product reaction norm kernels, a phenology-anchored neural network for time-resolved microbiome dynamics and a double hierarchical generalized linear model for stress-dependent residual variance). Of its four components, only the time-resolved microbiome modeling is methodologically new; the others apply established methods to a previously unaddressed combination. We also examine practical constraints such as data standardisation, computational cost, phenotyping infrastructure and accessibility and the ethical and regulatory questions that large-scale biological data integration raises. Realising this vision will require transdisciplinary collaboration among agronomists, soil biologists, crop physiologists, breeders and climate scientists.
