AB  - Effective feedback is crucial for learning, especially in the English as a Foreign Language (EFL) writing context. However, teacher feedback is often delayed and difficult to scale, while automated writing evaluation systems may struggle to address complex reasoning skills such as argumentation. Although large language models (LLMs) offer new possibilities because of their ability to interpret context, their feedback remains generic, lacks transparency, and can diminish students’ mental effort. To address these challenges, this study introduces a novel chain-of-metacognitive reasoning (CMR) framework, implemented through group relative policy optimization (GRPO) using reward functions. This training procedure was used to fine-tune a small-scale open-source LLM. The resulting CMR-GRPO system, ArguCoach, generates structured reasoning chains that assess argumentative writing. Unlike existing systems, ArguCoach drives the model to “think aloud,” modeling metacognitive strategies such as planning, monitoring, and evaluation. In the first stage, ArguCoach achieved higher agreement and fewer errors than did leading LLMs on over 5000 argumentative essays. In the second stage, undergraduate EFL students (n = 50) receiving ArguCoach feedback exhibited significantly greater writing performance and mental effort than did those (n = 60) with traditional teacher feedback in an eight-week quasi-experiment; notably, the writing performance gains persisted even after the intervention concluded. Feedback acceptance was similar across groups. However, students who received ArguCoach feedback were more likely to elaborate on suggestions with new content, whereas those who received teacher feedback were more likely to adopt suggestions directly. These findings indicate that aligning LLMs with metacognitive reasoning via reinforcement learning enables them to serve as effective partners in human–AI collaborative language education.
