AB  - Investor attention to biodiversity risk can alter investment behavior and, in turn, affect financial market risk. This paper provides global evidence on how investor attention to biodiversity risk affects corporate default risk and through which channels. Using news articles from leading media outlets in 13 countries over the 2018–2024 period, we apply Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) to construct a novel index of investor attention and examine its effect on corporate default risk. The results show that investors’ attention paid to news on biodiversity risk significantly expands firms’ distance to default, thereby reducing corporate default risk. Further analysis reveals an inverted U-shaped relationship between investor attention and distance to default, with the marginal risk-reducing effect diminishing beyond the turning point. Mechanism tests further show that investor attention to biodiversity risk reduces corporate default risk by improving market monitoring and risk pricing, whereas investor herding behavior, echo chamber effects, and higher firm-level environmental, social, and governance (ESG) performance significantly attenuate this risk-mitigating effect. The effect is stronger in more developed, institutionally robust, and economically free markets, as well as among firms with higher stock liquidity, but weaker for those with higher policy uncertainty, larger cash holdings, higher capital expenditure intensity, biodiversity-related industry exposure, greater global value chain stability, and during the post-Kunming-COP15 period. This study enriches the understanding of how biodiversity risk information affects corporate financial stability from a third-party news attention perspective and provides implications for sustainable financial regulation and investor risk management.
