AB  - Crop disease diagnosis from field photographs faces two recurring problems: models that score well on benchmarks frequently hallucinate species names, and when predictions are correct, the reasoning behind them is typically inaccessible to the practitioner. This paper describes Agri-CPJ (Caption-Prompt-Judge), a training-free few-shot framework in which a large vision-language model first generates a structured morphological caption, quality-gated and, when triggered, refined once, before any diagnostic question is answered. Two candidate responses are then generated from complementary viewpoints, and an LLM judge selects the stronger one based on domain-specific criteria. The ablations show that introducing a caption at all, even before any quality refinement, is the single largest contributor to downstream accuracy for both models tested; quality-gated refinement of that caption adds a smaller but systematic further increment. On CDDMBench, the full caption-enhanced pipeline yields +22.7 pp in disease classification and +19.5 points in QA score over no-caption baselines (GPT-5-Nano with GPT-5-mini caption generator), with ablations showing caption introduction accounts for the majority of the gain. Evaluated without modification on AgMMU-MCQs, GPT-5-Nano reached 77.84% and Qwen-VL-Chat reached 64.54%, placing them at or above most open-source models of comparable scale despite the format shift from open-ended to multiple-choice. The structured caption and judge rationale together constitute a readable intermediate record: a practitioner who disagrees with a diagnosis can inspect the specific caption observation the answer was conditioned on, though we make no claim that the judge’s written rationale is a faithful account of the computation that produced its selection. The code and scripts are publicly available.11https://github.com/CPJ-Agricultural/CPJ-Agricultural-Diagnosis.
