AB  - Intent Based Networking (IBN) using LLMs promises to automate high-level operational intent and translate it into vendor-specific Command Line Interfaces (CLI) configurations. But deploying Large Language Models (LLMs) into mission-critical network infrastructure poses catastrophic failure modes that are simply at odds with the need for production reliability. These failures can be called Syntactic Fabrication because models can produce commands that exist in the CLI but are either misspelled or not listed anywhere in vendor documentation, and Version Pollution, in which commands from one version of the operating system are mistakenly combined with commands from another version, causing configuration rejection or dangerous prefix hijacking. This paper introduces RAG-NetTwin, a knowledge grounded IBN synthesis framework that results in a hallucination rate of 2.9% on an evaluation corpus of 35 intents, based on three core innovations: Hierarchical Graph-Augmented Retrieval (HGR) using a 1334-node knowledge graph with 162 functional communities, Mandatory Citation Hallucination Refinement (MCR) by iterative LLM auditing with mandatory citation binding, and a Three-Tier Validation pipeline incorporating Batfish Static Analysis, Referential Integrity checks, and GNS3 emulation. The system achieves an 82.9% in Aggregate Config Acceptance Rate (CAR) across both vendors [68.6%,94.3%], 100% Parameter Extraction Accuracy, and a 16.4x token reduction from God Node pruning and community budgeting. RAG-NetTwin outperforms vanilla LLM baselines, which validate 74.3% of the sentences syntactically and hallucinate 25.7%, by significantly improving reliability using deterministic validation at each synthesis step.
