AB  - As a cornerstone of bottom-up sustainability initiatives, education for sustainable development cultivates ecological stewardship in younger generations by fostering harmony between humans and nature. Despite existing efforts in primary-level sustainability education, the inherent complexity of this multidimensional concept poses significant pedagogical challenges due to its interdisciplinary knowledge requirements. Recently, the rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has driven transformative impacts across domains of Artificial Intelligence–Generated Content (AIGC). Leveraging the sustainability knowledge embedded in LLMs for education for sustainable development, we propose a three-phase workflow (interaction, generation, optimization) that transforms passive tools into an active pedagogical platform. Specifically, we introduce a cyclic generative LLM scheme that autonomously generates and optimizes multimedia content in a complementary manner without additional training. To ensure generation relevance and correctness, we further optimize sustainability-related content through hard constraints derived from knowledge graphs, where biased and ambiguous knowledge is mitigated by explicit vertex and edge restrictions. Finally, we design an LLM-centered interactive interface to realize interaction and feedback, enabling real-time responses and personalized learning pathways. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed framework outperforms existing methods in generative capability, while also exhibiting strong performance in usability and learnability.
