AB  - With individuals spending more than 85% of their time indoors, maintaining Thermal Comfort (TC) is essential for well-being and energy efficiency. While the Predicted Mean Vote (PMV) index is the standard metric, its accuracy suffers when personal factors, specifically Metabolic Rate (MET) and Clothing Insulation (CLO), are treated as constant values. Recent advances in Computer Vision (CV) offer a non-intrusive method to estimate these parameters, yet progress is hindered by the scarcity of privacy-compliant, granular real-world datasets. We introduce a novel high-fidelity synthetic dataset generated via Unreal Engine 5 (UE5), featuring 2250 video clips with expert-annotated ground truth for MET and CLO aligned with ASHRAE Standard 55. Leveraging this dataset, we present the first comprehensive benchmark comparing a specialized supervised model against state-of-the-art (SOTA) zero-shot Vision-Language Models (VLMs). We propose a modified pretrained multi-task Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architecture as a supervised baseline and contrast it with the flagship Gemini 3 Pro and the lightweight 2.5 Flash using a Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting strategy. Results indicate that zero-shot Gemini 3 Pro significantly outperforms the CNN baseline in semantic accuracy, achieving Mean Absolute Errors (MAEs) of 0.31 MET and 0.021 CLO. Conversely, latency analysis identifies the multi-task CNN as the most viable option for high-frequency control loops due to its 3.93-second inference latency per clip, while Gemini 2.5 Flash provides a balanced, accessible alternative for periodic building adjustments. This study validates the utility of synthetic digital occupants for benchmarking AI models, enabling a scalable, privacy-preserving Occupant Centric Control (OCC) pipeline.
