AB  - On construction sites, various dangerous operations are often unavoidably involved, necessitating intelligent risk identification and assessment as a critical component of safety management. This study proposes a novel approach that integrates knowledge graph (KG) and large language models (LLMs) as the core reasoning engine for construction safety risk assessment, enhanced by a graph-of-thought (GoT) process. First, a structured and computable safety knowledge graph is built as the core knowledge base. A Vision-Language Model then conducts multi-dimensional semantic analysis of construction images to create detailed descriptions. Next, guided by a task-specific GoT process, the system formulates risk-related queries and uses retrieval-augmented generation to extract relevant accident data and fault trees from the KG. By merging image semantics with structured knowledge and fault-tree reasoning, the framework allows for quantitative assessment of potential hazards. Ultimately, the LLM generates a standardized and easy-to-understand risk assessment report. A feedback loop involving expert review and real-world performance further improves the knowledge graph, fault-tree models, and query logic. Case studies of typical scenarios, such as work at heights and hot work operations, have validated the effectiveness of this framework in identifying risk factors, simulating accident scenarios, and assessing risk levels, thereby providing a proof-of-concept technical approach for the intelligent assessment of construction safety risks.
