KW  - Uncertainty quantification
KW  - Selective prediction
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Confidence calibration
KW  - Intrusion detection
KW  - Human–AI triage
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) can classify network flows for intrusion detection, but safe deployment depends less on raw accuracy than on knowing when to defer to a human: a model must flag which decisions to automate and which to escalate. We study whether black-box, prompt-based uncertainty quantification (UQ) can support such a selective-automation (accept/escalate/reject) policy. We present the first systematic comparison of seven prompt-based UQ methods across five LLMs and two benchmarks (CIC-IDS2017, CIC-IDS2018) with stratified sampling (n=750 per dataset), spanning self-report confidence, multi-prompt voting, Monte Carlo prompting, Cross-Variant Sampling, and three penalty-adjusted variants. Three findings emerge. First, the binding constraint on safe automation is classification accuracy, not calibration: with a mean accepted accuracy of 0.630 — well below the 0.95 operational target — even well-calibrated uncertainty cannot license autonomous acceptance, so UQ’s value here is escalation, not full automation. Second, Penalty-Adjusted Multi-Prompt Voting (PA-MPV) attains the lowest mean calibration error (ECE = 0.217) at zero additional API cost over plain multi-prompt voting (ECE = 0.243), whereas Monte Carlo prompting severely degrades calibration (ECE = 0.597); penalty adjustment lowers ECE for all base methods, but only deterministic ones beat baseline. The modest PA-MPV and MPV gains are not significant after multiple-comparison correction, whereas the stochastic-method degradations are. Third, reasoning-specialized models are more accurate than general-purpose models (0.675 versus 0.604), but their calibration advantage largely vanishes at scale. These results reframe prompt-based UQ as a triage mechanism—valuable for routing uncertain cases to human analysts, but not, at current accuracy, for unsupervised automation.
