AB  - In the red and yellow soil regions of southern China, soil acidification management is constrained by fragmented knowledge resources, high technical barriers, and uneven regional technical capacity, limiting the systematic integration and dissemination of management knowledge. To address these challenges, this study proposes a lightweight autonomous knowledge graph construction framework based on large language model (LLM) agents. The framework integrates an AI associative matrix and a semantic expansion-retrieval mechanism to infer concepts and relationships from a small set of domain-specific keywords, enabling autonomous generation of an initial ontology framework and knowledge graph schema. A domain-wide intelligence mining and multi-source heterogeneous data acquisition strategy based on deep web mining is further employed to automatically acquire and semantically process governmental documents, monitoring data, and other publicly available domain resources, while a cloud-edge collaborative architecture supports deployment on resource-constrained devices. The framework was benchmarked against a professionally constructed domain knowledge graph developed over two years by multidisciplinary experts and compared with conventional knowledge graph construction and general few-shot learning-based extraction approaches across six dimensions: data preparation, construction cycle, technical threshold, dynamic evolution, target users and scenarios, and training requirements. The proposed framework demonstrated advantages across these dimensions while achieving a precision of 60.67 %, recall of 74.16 %, and F1-score of 66.74 %, demonstrating the feasibility of lightweight autonomous knowledge extraction. In-situ deployment in Chengmai County, Hainan Province, further demonstrated stable operation on low-resource mobile terminals under fluctuating rural network conditions. User evaluations indicated adaptability across different technical backgrounds and resource-input levels, while the extensible architecture supports the integration of emerging agricultural technologies and updated policy regulations. Overall, this study provides a lightweight, user-friendly, and scalable approach to autonomous knowledge graph construction, with potential applications in cultivated land quality improvement and agricultural ecological management.
