KW  - Fake news detection
KW  - Multimodal feature extraction
KW  - Multimodal feature fusion
KW  - Multimodal fake news detection
AB  - The rise of social media has accelerated the proliferation of fake news. Although multimodal learning has significantly advanced Fake News Detection (FND), the research community has produced few comprehensive surveys that jointly analyze the full process of Multimodal Fake News Detection (MFND). To address this gap, this paper reviews recent advancements, key approaches, and challenges in MFND. Specifically, we introduce a pipeline- and interaction-oriented taxonomy that organizes existing methods into three stages: multimodal feature extraction, multimodal feature fusion, and multimodal fake news classification. For feature extraction, we review unimodal, cross-modal, and Large Language Model/Large Vision-Language Model (LLM/LVLM)-based methods. For feature fusion, we analyze shallow fusion, deep fusion, hybrid fusion, and LLM/LVLM-based fusion. For classification, we summarize Machine Learning (ML)-based, Deep Learning (DL)-based, and LLM/LVLM-based methods. Within this framework, we further examine literature-informed interactions among feature quality, cross-modal alignment, fusion reliability, representation refinement, and classification feedback in practical MFND systems. We also examine experimental settings, dataset usage, performance differences, dataset biases, reproducibility issues, and cross-study comparability across representative studies. Finally, we identify unresolved challenges and outline five future directions: implicit semantic feature extraction, real-time adaptive multimodal fusion, dynamic and robust fake news classification, LLM/LVLM-based MFND in the era of Generative AI, and robust MFND under missing modalities and noisy data. This survey establishes a systematic and interactive research framework for MFND and offers methodological guidance for designing effective and trustworthy MFND systems.
